## Why This Is Asked

This problem evaluates the ability to design a dynamic programming solution for selecting exactly k vertex-disjoint directed paths in a DAG while maximizing total node weights. It is a common pattern in interviews at Netflix, Uber, and Meta.

## Key Concepts

- **Topological ordering** ensures DP processes vertices in valid dependency order
- **DP over path count dimension** (t from 1 to k) tracks the maximum weight for t paths ending at each vertex
- **Vertex-disjoint constraint** is naturally enforced through the state definition, since each path is built incrementally
- **Parent pointer array** enables reconstruction of the selected paths after DP computation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code for k vertex-disjoint path selection

function selectKPaths(N, M, edges, weights, k) {
  const dp = Array(N).fill(null).map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array(N).fill(null).map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

  // Get topological order
  const topo = topologicalSort(N, edges);

  // Initialize sources
  for (const s of sources) {
    dp[s][1] = weights[s];
  }

  // Process in topological order
  for (const u of topo) {
    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      for (let t = 1; t < k; t++) {
        if (dp[u][t] > -Infinity) {
          const newWeight = dp[u][t] + weights[v];
          if (newWeight > dp[v][t + 1]) {
            dp[v][t + 1] = newWeight;
            parent[v][t + 1] = u;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct paths using parent pointers
  return reconstructPaths(dp, parent, k);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine subarray maxima with partition DP, requiring both maximization and reconstruction of multiple intervals. It exercises careful indexing and boundary handling.

## Key Concepts
- Non-overlapping subarrays with a cap k
- Precomputation of maxSub for O(1) subarray maxima
- 2D DP with reconstruction pointers
- Time/space tradeoffs and edge cases (k=0, equal values)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValueWithKSubarrays(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const maxSub = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    let m = -Infinity;
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      m = Math.max(m, A[r]);
      maxSub[l][r] = m;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(0));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      let best = dp[i-1][t];
      let bestJ = -1;
      for (let j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
        const val = maxSub[j-1][i-1] + dp[j-1][t-1];
        if (val > best) { best = val; bestJ = j; }
      }
      dp[i][t] = best; take[i][t] = bestJ;
    }
  }
  const res = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && i > 0) {
    const j = take[i][t];
    if (j === -1) break;
    res.push([j-1, i-1]);
    i = j - 1;
    t--;
  }
  res.reverse();
  return {value: dp[n][k], subarrays: res};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over prefixes with a non-linear subarray cost (max) and requires reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes and partition count
- On-the-fly max for subarray cost
- Reconstruction via prev pointers
- Time/space trade-offs (O(n^2 k) time, O(nk) space)

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const INF = 1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(INF));
  const prev = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    for(let t=1; t<=Math.min(i,k); t++){
      let curMax = -Infinity;
      for(let p=i-1; p>=t-1; p--){
        curMax = Math.max(curMax, A[p]);
        const val = dp[p][t-1] + curMax;
        if(val < dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; prev[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  return {cost: dp[n][k], prev};
}
```

